---

<section id="learn" class="seo" aria-labelledby="seo-heading">
  <div class="container-page seo__inner">
    <header class="seo__head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The guide</span>
      <h2 id="seo-heading" class="seo__title">
        Understanding your financial health score
      </h2>
      <p class="seo__lead">
        Your money picture is more than one account balance. A financial health
        score pulls savings, debt, income, and net worth into a single number
        you can actually act on — here's how ours works.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="seo__prose">
      <h3>What is a financial health score?</h3>
      <p>
        A financial health score is a single, easy-to-read number that captures
        how strong your overall money situation is. Rather than checking a
        savings balance here and a loan statement there, this kind of personal
        finance score blends the most important signals — what you have saved,
        what you owe, and how that compares to people in your stage of life — into
        one benchmark. Think of it as a money health check or financial checkup:
        a quick read on your financial wellness that you can track over time and
        compare against realistic targets.
      </p>

      <h3>How the Financial Cooked Score works</h3>
      <p>
        The Cooked Score is our financial fitness calculator output, expressed on
        a simple 0–100 scale. It is built from four factors, weighted by how much
        they actually reflect long-term financial health:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Savings-to-income ratio</strong> — the biggest driver. More
          savings relative to your income means a stronger cushion and a higher
          score.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Debt-to-income ratio</strong> — the second-heaviest factor.
          Carrying a lot of debt for your income pulls the score down.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Net savings</strong> — your savings and investments minus total
          debt, the foundation of your net worth score.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Age-adjusted progress</strong> — how your savings stack up
          against typical milestones for your age.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because savings and debt carry the most weight, this is not a tool where a
        big paycheck alone wins. A high earner who saves little can still score
        below a modest earner with a healthy emergency fund and little debt — which
        is exactly how real financial wellness works.
      </p>

      <h3>What Financial Age means</h3>
      <p>
        Financial Age is the headline result of our financial age calculator. It
        translates your savings progress into an age: if you have banked more than
        is typical for someone your age, your financial age comes out younger than
        your real age. If you are behind on savings or weighed down by debt, it
        reads older. It is an intuitive way to see whether you are ahead of or
        behind the curve on retirement readiness without wading through
        spreadsheets.
      </p>

      <h3>How percentile comparisons work</h3>
      <p>
        Numbers mean more in context, so the results include a percentile that
        shows how you compare to other Americans your age. This financial
        percentile calculator maps your net worth onto age-based benchmarks
        inspired by the Federal Reserve's Survey of Consumer Finances, then
        estimates the share of peers you are financially ahead of — for example,
        "ahead of 68% of Americans your age." It turns an abstract score into a
        clear sense of where you stand.
      </p>

      <h3>How savings, debt, and income affect your score</h3>
      <p>
        The three levers behave differently. <strong>Savings</strong> have the
        largest positive impact: every bit you set aside improves your
        savings-to-income ratio and your net worth. <strong>Debt</strong> works in
        the opposite direction — a high debt-to-income ratio, or owing more than
        you have saved, drags both your score and your percentile down.
        <strong>Income</strong> plays a supporting role: it makes a given amount of
        debt more manageable, but it never guarantees a good score on its own. The
        fastest way to climb is almost always to grow savings and pay down
        high-interest debt.
      </p>

      <h3>Using your results</h3>
      <p>
        Treat your Cooked Score as a starting point for a financial health
        assessment, not a verdict. Run the check, note your score, percentile, and
        financial age, then revisit after a few months or a major change — a raise,
        a new loan, a fully funded emergency fund. Watching the number move is the
        most motivating part: as you save more and owe less, your financial fitness
        improves and the score follows.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

